WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.490
It's a Saturday for me in Los Angeles,

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.680
and because I am a total space nerd

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.470
I've come to North
Hollywood to Norton Sales.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:16.500
This place is legendary
in the aerospace industry.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.360
For decades they've been hoarding

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.673
any sort of space junk they could get.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.660
No less than a young pup of an Elon Musk

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.420
and a equally puppy Peter
Beck from Rocket Lab

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.843
came here before they started
their giant space companies.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.760
Can look around, find
a valve, many valves,

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.593
a turbo pump, whatever you need.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:47.310
If you've seen the Mandalorian,
Star Trek, Star Wars,

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.800
pretty good chance they
picked up a few objects

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.500
from this place.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.500
Three, two, one, zero, liftoff.

00:00:56.613 --> 00:00:58.091
We have a liftoff.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.650
If you too, want to get
space junk, you should come,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.413
and if nothing else, you can sit in a pod.

00:01:22.016 --> 00:01:25.773
Los Angeles's aerospace
history goes back 100 years.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.603
Some of the first plane
companies were here.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.893
Some of the first rocket
experimenters too.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.233
LA helped turn the US into
an aerospace superpower.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.743
The old guard is still here.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.230
But now there's Elon Musk's SpaceX

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:55.140
and a half dozen more rocket startups.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.690
Not to mention myriad more new satellite

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.793
and space manufacturing companies.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.110
SpaceX has turned into the towering giant

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.090
of commercial space.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.420
And many of its spawn have
turned LA into the centre

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.343
of the commercial space revolution.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.770
As luck would have it,

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:17.400
I know just the man to
explain how this happened

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.323
and where this is all heading.

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:23.700
If you are a nerd like me,
LA can be a magical place.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.950
You can be by an oil refinery

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.340
and then a space startup at the same time.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.410
I'm here to meet Tom Mueller.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:34.680
He's the CEO of a startup
called Impulse Space.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.010
He's the dude who built the
first engines for SpaceX.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.500
He's a gear head. He's my kind of people.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.233
And I think you all should come meet Tom.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.950
This was the engine that
I designed, actually,

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.218
before we even incorporated Impulse.

00:02:53.218 --> 00:02:54.308
This was just floating
around in your head?

00:02:54.308 --> 00:02:56.130
Just floating around.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.290
I don't know. It just
snowballed and became a company.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.123
Yeah.

00:02:59.123 --> 00:03:01.260
Here we go. We're off and running.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.460
Can we check out this guy?

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:03.293
Yeah.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.023
My relationship with Tom
goes something like this:

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.800
He makes cool space hardware,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.880
then tries to explain it to
me while I nod and smile.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.250
If you, if you burn
past that, you have what

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.480
what are called cosine losses.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:22.313
Okay.

00:03:22.313 --> 00:03:24.630
So you just do a whole
bunch of short burns

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.030
like when you're doing
the Holman transfers

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.660
you have a huge float. Say
you're going from Leo to Geo,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.900
and you got to do

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.610
Well, that's going to take, you know-

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.100
The man has a beautiful mind

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:41.430
and after 18 years
sweating it out under Elon,

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.940
he started his own space company, Impulse.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.750
I will show you its wonders
in a minute, but first,

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.190
Tom offered to drive me around town

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.473
in one of his many fast cars.

00:03:59.776 --> 00:04:00.609
Oh yeah.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.663
In addition to being a rocket
God, he's an amateur racer.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.123
Yes.

00:04:19.227 --> 00:04:21.810
How many tickets have you gotten in LA?

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:24.843
I haven't had any lately,
but I've gotten quite a few.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.610
So the racing, you just

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.510
jumped into it and you just-

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:35.130
Yeah. It's pretty addicting.
You wouldn't be doing it

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.480
unless it was fun because
it's crazy expensive.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.710
He may spend his spare
time racing Porsches now,

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.233
but Tom comes from humble roots.

00:04:47.779 --> 00:04:50.100
You're an Idaho man originally.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.350
Yeah. Born and raised.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:56.250
Me and my brother were pretty
wild riding bikes, hiking,

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.650
going out in the woods
and playing all the time.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:03.630
When did it become obvious

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.240
to you that you were going
to build rocket engines?

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.580
I forget where I saw it,
of course, but, you know,

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.460
in some magazine or
something, you fill out a form

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.050
and you order these Estes rockets

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:14.880
and wait weeks for it to come.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:15.713
The model rockets.

00:05:15.713 --> 00:05:17.010
Yeah. The little model rockets.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:17.843
Yeah, the kits.

00:05:17.843 --> 00:05:19.950
The little kits. And I
built a lot of those.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:21.930
And then I got into high school

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.147
and took a math class
and my math teacher said,

00:05:25.147 --> 00:05:26.520
"Wow, Tom, you're really good at math.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.630
Yeah. You're going to
be an engineer, right?"

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.340
I go, "No, I'm going to
be an aircraft mechanic."

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.470
He's like, "Do you want to
be the guy that fixes a plane

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.675
or the guy that designs a plane?"

00:05:33.675 --> 00:05:35.125
That totally changed my life.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.970
When did you first move to LA?

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:48.963
1985.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:53.400
The aerospace industry has
had so many ups and downs.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.325
When you arrived, was it a boom cycle or?

00:05:56.325 --> 00:05:58.140
It was a boom, it was the Reagan era.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.993
Star Wars and all that was going on.

00:05:59.993 --> 00:06:00.826
Yeah.

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:01.659
So it was the good times.

00:06:01.659 --> 00:06:02.576
Yeah.

00:06:02.576 --> 00:06:03.750
Yeah.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.730
Tom worked for the next decade and a half

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.733
at companies like Hughes Aircraft and TRW,

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:15.420
but then LA's space scene,
and the space scene in general

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:17.280
hit a major funk.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.870
Innovation stalled,

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:22.143
government and military
bureaucracy reigned supreme.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:31.860
And then out of nowhere, the
PayPal guy turned up in LA

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.840
and started talking about his dream

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.540
to build a colony on the red planet.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:38.040
I don't think we should
be going back to the moon.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.380
I think we should be focused on Mars.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:42.690
Half the people, well, more than half

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:45.960
would be telling this guy,
Elon, that that's crazy.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.610
But then there's, there's
going to be this group

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:49.491
of people that want to
give it a go, right?

00:06:49.491 --> 00:06:50.324
Yeah.

00:06:50.324 --> 00:06:51.180
And you, you end up as one of those.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:55.320
Yep. Yeah, I think there's
a lot of naysayers.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.840
I was not able to hire
very many people early on

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.690
because they just didn't think
a small company could do it.

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:04.590
Right? But Elon, you know,
he just has this spirit like,

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.740
we're going to do it,
come hell or high water.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:12.540
Alright, now this is the
building right up here.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.250
The first SpaceX building?

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.083
Yeah.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.940
Oh, it is a fish place now.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:23.933
How long were you guys here?

00:07:23.933 --> 00:07:25.170
It was the first what?

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:28.080
I think we moved in at about June of 2002.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.130
So it was five years.

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.370
The machine shop was
straight back here and

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.670
we built the first Falcon
9, first stage in here

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.920
and famously had to
remove all the landscaping

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.210
to get it out.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:40.170
So shouldn't this be

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:42.942
a historical landmark or something?

00:07:42.942 --> 00:07:45.092
Yeah. It's funny that it's a fish market.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:07:59.970
Traditional aerospace
folks thought of SpaceX

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:03.030
as a complete joke and
the company had plenty

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:05.013
of struggles during its first years.

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:10.023
Still, Tom and the rest of
the SpaceX team kept at it.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.000
They built their engines
named Merlin and Kestrel

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.393
in California, and then
commuted to Texas to test them.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:22.743
Sometimes they worked,
sometimes they didn't.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:28.473
In 2008, all the hard work paid
off in spectacular fashion.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:32.550
SpaceX made it to orbit
with a liquid fueled rocket,

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:35.370
a first for a private rocket company.

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:37.530
Elon always knew that we were
going to be super successful.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:39.930
Like he always said, someday
our name's going to be

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:40.763
as big as Boeing's.

00:08:40.763 --> 00:08:42.382
And I'm like, yeah, yeah, right.

00:08:42.382 --> 00:08:46.230
Holy. He was right.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:48.630
Tom didn't make much money at SpaceX,

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.280
but his pride and his accomplishments

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:54.930
keeps him warm at night.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:56.880
Stop it, hit the middle button

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.913
and then you hit it and
it'll go the other way.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.313
Just kidding. He's loaded.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:07.110
This is when you know you've made it,

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.420
when you've got a Ferrari
spinning around in here.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:10.253
I'm blessed.

00:09:10.253 --> 00:09:11.086
In your garage.

00:09:11.086 --> 00:09:12.393
SpaceX has been very good to me.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.480
Are you worried that you have a problem?

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.180
Oh, I definitely have a problem.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:23.910
Then there's the decked out home workshop

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:27.870
where Tom can relive his glory
days flying hobbyist rockets.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.750
So this is probably the smallest
liquid rocket ever flown.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:33.720
25 pounds of thrust, inch
and a half in diameter.

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:35.010
It's kind of like a cool part of

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:36.450
southern California though, is this

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.760
whole thing where you can go from LA

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.910
to Mojave and just go
do whatever you want.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:43.936
Two. One. Ignition

00:09:47.677 --> 00:09:48.510
Looks like we might be able to

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:50.670
salvage the engine on this even.

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:52.050
Look what came out of it, you know?

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:54.090
Because I was known for making nice parts,

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:56.640
when Elon came and he knew
some people in the club,

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:58.050
he said "Hey, I need an engine guy."

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:00.400
They recommended me and
that's how it happened.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:03.720
Alright. We got to be careful.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.520
This is a construction zone out here

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:10.383
And of course the greatest
man cave of all time.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:16.170
So this is the Merlin 1A.

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:18.030
So this was the original flight engine

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.710
that we flew on the first two flights.

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:20.940
I know every part of this thing.

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:22.710
I've machined panel tips for this thing,

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:24.243
and we lived this engine.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.240
The room is filled with the spoils

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:30.090
of Tom's four decade career in aerospace.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.700
This right here is a LEM D injector.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:35.520
This is what they landed on the moon.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:36.600
When Armstrong was throttling,

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:37.590
trying to find a place to land,

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:39.150
he was throttling this engine.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:40.913
Eagle, looking great. You're go.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:43.170
If you're anything like me,

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:46.050
you could spend days
here, but there's no time

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:48.840
to hang around with relics of the past.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:52.263
Next stop LA's Aerospace future.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:04.263
Right when I started
covering SpaceX, it was like,

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.280
the US aerospace industry's
really sad and depressing

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:10.080
and young people didn't
want to work there.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.130
And then you guys were at
it for now these 20 years,

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:18.240
but now there's no more
exciting country anywhere.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:19.590
Not only do we have SpaceX,

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:21.330
but we've got small rocket makers,

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:22.950
we've got tons of satellites.

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:25.980
Everything just the
flywheel effect of SpaceX

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:28.920
has started the space -
Really this is the modern era.

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:30.620
The space age started right there.

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:37.050
Now that companies like SpaceX have made

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:39.690
getting off-planet relatively affordable,

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:42.310
space ventures are popping up all over LA

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:46.413
cooking up wild ideas to try out in orbit.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:52.400
Like Varda Space Industries run
by William here, a spaceX ex.

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:54.960
They set up shop a couple of years ago

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:57.390
in this former skateboard warehouse.

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:59.880
Varda is the first in-space manufacturing

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:01.470
and earth re-entry company.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:03.810
So what we do is we take
advantage of microgravity to

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:06.300
make unique products since
it's a unique environment

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.580
and then we sell those
products back on earth.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:09.870
People have talked about this for years,

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:11.970
but you're really the first
that are going to try to

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:13.800
do manufacturing in space.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:16.260
Yeah, exactly. So it's not my idea.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:18.270
It's been around for decades.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:20.130
People have just been
waiting for launch costs to

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:23.253
drop low enough to make
this economically feasible.

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:29.460
Varda's first space manufacturing project

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.683
will be essentially molecules.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.280
It's been shown that
novel atomic structures

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.650
can be formed in microgravity.

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:40.260
The hope is that more
useful things will result,

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:42.540
like new life-saving medicines.

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:43.860
The pharmaceutical industry just thinks

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:46.320
of us as a bioreactor and
then when they walk away,

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:47.850
we just take and we put
it into space real quick

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:48.810
and then we bring it back.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:51.090
And that's equivalent to
turning the gravity knob

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:54.360
off for that chamber while we
run that automated process.

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:58.620
You guys also have plans for,
possibly, giant factories.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:01.530
That's more than like the
10 to 20 year timeframe.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:03.960
The first thing you'll
see is just rapid cadence

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:07.410
of small launch vehicles to
take advantage of microgravity.

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:09.570
The bet that we're making as a company is

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:12.960
that manufacturing comes before tourism,

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.800
and as the next step of
commercialization of the cosmos.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.590
For now, Varda's molecular
magic will take place

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:22.803
in this small capsule that
they call the bird bath.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:26.130
So this one has served its purpose.

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:29.040
It's been on a parachute
drop test quite a few times

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:32.973
and this is kind of the
dilapidated test vehicle.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:39.840
Inside the capsule is where

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:41.673
the weightless chemistry happens.

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:46.020
I would love to show you
what that looks like, but...

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:47.100
We can't do the payload. Yeah, yeah.

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:48.197
So that's secret.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:52.143
Which means it's time for
our recurring segment:

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.393
A child draws the proprietary technology.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:01.140
Can you describe it?

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:02.058
Yes.

00:14:02.058 --> 00:14:03.030
In as much detail, and we will create

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:04.427
an artist representation.

00:14:04.427 --> 00:14:05.260
Yeah. Yeah, yeah.

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:07.260
Hi, my name is Riaan Shah.

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:10.050
I'm going to draw some
pictures for you today.

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:13.500
